# PHP Architecture

Homes for PHP, chosen by shape rather than by topic.

| Shape | Home |
|---|---|
| Stateful feature that registers hooks | `lib/class-{name}.php`, a `Happywood\` class |
| REST endpoint | `public/class-api-{concern}.php`, extending `Api` |
| Procedural functions for one capability | `includes/{capability}/{name}-functions.php` |
| Hook wiring for a capability | `includes/{capability}/{name}-hooks.php` |
| Genuinely generic machinery, no capability | `includes/{name}-functions.php` (root) |
| Third-party plugin integration | `includes/integrations/{plugin}.php` |

## Classes

```php
<?php

namespace Happywood;

class Custom_Feature {

	public function __construct() {
		$this->register_hooks();
	}

	private function register_hooks(): void {
		add_action( 'init', [ $this, 'do_thing' ] );
	}

	public function do_thing(): void {
		// ...
	}

}
```

- **File name must match the class name exactly.** `composer.json` uses a
  `classmap` autoloader over `lib` and `public` — not PSR-4. Case-sensitive
  filesystems will fail silently on a mismatch.
- Hooks are registered in a private `register_hooks()` called from the
  constructor. Nothing registers hooks at file scope.
- Use WordPress default priorities unless there is a stated reason not to.
- Run `composer dump-autoload` after adding a class.

Instantiate from `lib/class-init.php`, never anywhere else:

```php
// lib/class-init.php
public function __construct() {
	// ...existing calls...
	$this->define_custom_feature_hooks();
}

private function define_custom_feature_hooks(): void {
	new Custom_Feature();
}
```

Guard classes that depend on a plugin, so the theme degrades instead of fataling:

```php
public function __construct() {
	if ( ! class_exists( 'WooCommerce' ) ) {
		return;
	}
	// ...
}
```

## REST endpoints

The one exception to `lib/`. Endpoints live in `public/class-api-{concern}.php`
and extend the abstract `Api` base class, which owns the `happywood/v1`
namespace, the `rest_api_init` wiring, and response shaping. A subclass declares
routes in `register_routes()` and nothing else.

**`public/` holds endpoint classes and nothing else.** Not front-end rendering,
not asset enqueuing — those are `lib/`. The name describes the HTTP surface, not
the audience; read as "public-facing" it turns into a second junk drawer.

Never write `register_rest_route()` into an `includes/` function file. That was
the old pattern and it fataled a fork that deleted the owning capability.

**Full convention: `.cursor/rules/sf-rest-api.mdc`** — the base class members, the
`is_available()` versus `permission_callback` split, registration from
`Init::define_api_hooks()`, and the nonce contract for JS callers. Read it before
adding or changing an endpoint.

## Function files

`require_once` new files from that capability's `bootstrap.php`. `includes/init.php`
is a map of those bootstraps plus the timeless root files — do not add a
capability file to `init.php` directly. Header and menu bootstraps are loaded
from their `lib/` classes, not from `init.php`.

`includes/` has two tiers:

```
includes/
  init.php  constants.php  helper-functions.php     ← timeless, never deletable
  accordion-functions.php  taxonomy-terms-functions.php
  category-swiper-functions.php  load-more-functions.php
  archive-shell-functions.php

  woocommerce/  post/  faq/  showroom/              ← capabilities, each with bootstrap.php
  header/  menu/  integrations/                     ← header/menu bootstraps load from lib/
```

Root `load-more-functions.php` is generic (per-page helper and filters). Catalog
per-page, orderby, and shop query gates live in
`includes/woocommerce/load-more-functions.php`. Unscoped `/?s=` product scoping
lives in `includes/woocommerce/search-hooks.php`. Do not put those branches back
in the timeless tier.

**Default to a capability directory.** The root tier is only for machinery with no
capability of its own — accordions, taxonomy term lookups, generic swipers. A
capability directory plus its one `bootstrap.php` require is deleted as a unit; a file at
the root can never be deleted, so a capability-specific file placed there welds
that capability into the starter for good.

**Name directories after the capability, never the layer.** `woocommerce/`,
`showroom/`, `faq/` are right. `helpers/`, `utils/`, `services/`, `shared/` are
not — a layer directory grows into a grab-bag that no project can remove.

`includes/helper-functions.php` is for genuinely generic utilities only — build
paths, array and string helpers, debug. If a function calls a plugin's API, it
belongs in a capability directory. Current inventory:
`context/kb/map-helpers.md`.

Wrap redefinable helpers so a child theme or plugin can pre-empt them:

```php
if ( ! function_exists( 'happywood_write_log' ) ) {
	function happywood_write_log( $log ): void { /* ... */ }
}
```

## Naming

- Functions and hooks: `happywood_` prefix
- Constants: `HAPPYWOOD_` prefix, defined in `includes/constants.php`
- Nonces and custom hook names: build from `HAPPYWOOD_PREFIX` rather than
  hardcoding, so a rename stays mechanical

```php
wp_create_nonce( HAPPYWOOD_PREFIX . 'custom_action' );
```

## Checking before adding

Search before writing a helper — this theme descends from a starter whose
documentation described functions that were never ported. Confirm a function
exists in the codebase rather than trusting a reference to it.

`context/kb/map-helpers.md` inventories the generic helpers and lists the
ones documentation has claimed but that do not exist. It is generated from code;
regenerate it rather than trusting a stale copy.

What loads today: `context/kb/map-load.md`.

## File size

Review a new or growing hand-written file once it passes about **300–400
lines**, unless it is generated (`build/`, `build-jsx-blocks/`, icon font
output). That is a review prompt, not a lint gate. Split by responsibility
the way cart, catalog filters, reviews, and showroom singles already do —
keep public function and `init*` names so callers do not move.

